38 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517443 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517453 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517462 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517464 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517469 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186517474 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157438 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157527 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "986c9303-5c90-5d19-85f8-915c37c8bfb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158376 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158376 #edb/ref 1061 467336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158376 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56edca54-b7a1-571d-b42c-21eaa3456fa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158376 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158377 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158377 #edb/ref 1061 467337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158377 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f532c29e-5c06-5b5f-8c3b-8a42ad9ae43e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158377 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158378 #edb/ref 1061 467340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158378 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "942b92f8-26b9-51c3-94de-c738c9f145af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158378 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158530 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nx \\cdot 3^x - 2 \\cdot 3^x = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {3}^{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx-2  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^x=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x-2=0$ has the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation $3^x=0$ has no real solution because $3^x$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has a single solution, $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158530 #edb/ref 1065 4715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158530 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90c9dc44-008d-5e8d-b233-b33802547a9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158530 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171743 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171743 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equation of the parabola $y=2x^2 - 12x + 2$ in vertex form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "108ee1b6-2266-5956-b06d-4f4092fdee57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171743 #edb/ref 1074 "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square.\n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 12x + 2 = 2(x^2 - 6x + 1)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 1 &= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 1 \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} - 6x + 1) &= 2((x - 3)^{2} - 8) \\\\\n&= 2(x - 3)^{2} - 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny=2(x-3)^2 - 16\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171825 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171825 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is $(1, 2)$ and whose leading coefficient is $5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a48df2c-c48e-5862-884f-3768abfbe5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171825 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nand $a=5$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 5(x - 1)^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 5(x^{2} - 2x + 1) + 2 \\\\\n&= 5x^{2} - 10x + 5 + 2 \\\\\n&= 5x^{2} - 10x + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny = 5x^2-10x+7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171826 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171826 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the parabola $y=x^2-20x+20$ in vertex form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b2a8d64-5e70-598c-8baa-f7eb97ed0daf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171826 #edb/ref 1074 "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} - 20x + 20 \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 10 \\cdot x + 20 \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 10 \\cdot x + 10^{2} - 10^{2} + 20 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 10 \\cdot x + 10^{2}) - 10^{2} + 20 \\\\\n&= (x - 10)^{2} - 10^{2} + 20 \\\\\n&= (x - 10)^{2} - 100 + 20 \\\\\n&= (x - 10)^{2} - 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny=(x-10)^2 - 80\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171829 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171829 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{4x-5} - 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64957847-edce-52b1-9848-545c18014262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171829 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the roots of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{4x-5} - 3 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{4x - 5} - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{4x - 5} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{4x - 5})^{3} &= 3^{3} \\\\\n4x - 5 &= 27 \\\\\n4x &= 32 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171830 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171830 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = 3\\sqrt[4]{15- 8x} + 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96c06afd-4449-594e-8655-63f43ede736c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171830 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[4]{15- 8x} + 3 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sqrt[4]{15 - 8x} + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n3\\sqrt[4]{15 - 8x} &= -3 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{15 - 8x} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172023 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3e6159dd-c12e-5882-be17-815b4cc9ae52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172024